  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Noreaster will impact at least Downeast Maine\nthis weekend into Monday and possibly Tuesday, bringing gusty \nwinds, high surf, and periods of rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWe are still expecting a strong Noreaster to impact our area\nfrom through the weekend and into Monday. However, we \nwill be on the far northern fringe of the Noreaster with the \ncenter staying south of Massachusetts through Sunday, and \nimpacts in Northern regions through Sunday will be much less than \nareas further south. Here is what to expect for the various \ncomponents of the storm:\n\nPeriods of Rain...Rain chances will be far greater Downeast than\nnorthern areas for the storm, with rain possibly developing late\ntoday, and persisting off and on through Monday and possibly \neven Tuesday. Short range model guidance has adjusted the track \nof the system to remain much further south than previous \nthought. As a result, periods of rain should reach the Downeast \ncoast by late this afternoon, extending across Bangor and the \ninterior Downeast by tonight. Rain fall totals have coalesced \nsomewhat in with the updated track. Precipitation amounts \nbetween 0.25 inches and 1 inch are expected across the Bangor \nregion with localized totals up to 1.5 inches along the coast. \nMuch of this will wait until Sunday night and Monday to occur. \nEven with these totals, we would not expect flooding. Sunday \nnight and Monday, a much weaker version of the Noreaster finally\nlifts north and tracks into the Gulf of Maine, at which point \nrain chances and totals finally increase over the whole area. \nNorthern Maine could finally see some rain late Monday into \nTuesday, but it looks like it will be fairly light with maybe \naround one quarter inch.\n\nGusty Winds...Winds from the northeast will increase throughout\nthe day today, before becoming gusty by the afternoon and \nthrough Sunday, then decrease a bit into Monday. Winds will be \nstronger the further south you go but will top out around 25 mph\nacross Bangor and the Downeast with the aforementioned \nsoutherly track of the system, with isolated higher amounts \nalong the coast. We don't anticipate impacts from the wind other\nthan maybe a very isolated outage Downeast. \n\nHigh Surf...Seas near the shore will be fairly rough with 4-8 \nft seas, much of it as a longer period south/southeast swell. We\nare not expecting impactful coastal flooding, though waves \nbreaking on the rocks will be hazardous for any onlookers. \nConditions should be a bit more hazardous for coastal Hancock \nthan coastal Washington. Conditions slowly ease Sunday night \ninto Monday.",
